12777: # THE flush (codex-telemetry.ps1 -Flush; the detached sender): under the sender lock
12778: # (Enter-TelemetryFlushLock - a concurrent sender is refused, a stale lock taken over), every spool
12779: # file oldest first: lines older than 7 days and lines that are not spool lines are dropped; events go
12780: # in batches of at most 100 ({"events": [...]}) to <base>/v2/events, complaints one by one to
12781: # <base>/v2/complaints; the delivered and dropped lines are removed, the rest stays.
12782: # (wave 28b, D2) One flush ends after 60 s in all (TEST HOOK, test mode only:
12783: # CODEX_CONSULT_TEST_TELEMETRY_FLUSH_MS), one request after 8 s (CODEX_CONSULT_TEST_TELEMETRY_REQUEST_MS)
12784: # - what is not sent stays in the spool. (D8) Against the intake as it is built: a batch refused with
12785: # 400 "events[i]: <reason>" - event i is DROPPED (one line in .last `rejected`) and the rest resent, at
12786: # most three times per flush; 413 - the batch is halved (an event refused alone is dropped); 403 - the
12787: # flush stops, the spool is kept, the reason is said; any other 4xx, a 5xx, a timeout - the flush
12788: # stops there (nothing is hammered; the next run's sender tries again). The events not spooled since
12789: # the last flush (D6) are counted from zero again.
12790: # (wave 28c, D5 / F42-8) The 60 s cover the WHOLE flush - the lock, the spool's enumeration, every
